@flexiarg snatch/preserve-the-right-to-abstain
@title Preserve the Right to Abstain
@audience institution designers, rule authors, operators arming flags
@tone cautionary
@style pattern
@why snatch/protect-the-unprotected-move snatch/institutions-vary-by-position-and-force

! conclusion: Keep abstention available, and make any compulsion opt-in and visible.

  + context: Snatch or Share, variant G2 ("regla contraproductiva"). P2 holds a
    checkbox, *"Forzar oferta"*, which is **active by default**; when set, P1
    must propose and the "No ofrecer" button is disabled. Nothing else about the
    round changes. The variant is named counterproductive because the rule reads
    as encouraging exchange and in fact removes the exposed party's only
    protection.

  + IF:
    A rule is proposed that increases participation in an exchange.

  + HOWEVER:
    Compelling the exposed party to act removes the one move that protected
    them — declining — and a rule that is enabled by default is not experienced
    as a choice by anyone.

  + THEN:
    Leave abstention reachable, and where compulsion is wanted make it opt-in and
    visibly attributable to the party who imposed it.

  + BECAUSE:
    The option to decline is what prices an exchange. Remove it and the offer
    carries no information about willingness — only about obligation.
    + evidence: G2 is the variant the designers labelled counterproductive, and
      the only one whose added rule is on by default.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    - Ask of any default-on rule in the stack which move it removes, and from whom.
